Provenance of the interface vocabulary
======================================

Xpost's user-visible names come from three places, kept deliberately
distinct. This note records which is which, and why the third group
exists, so the interoperability intent is documented rather than
implicit.


== 1. Adobe-published specifications ==

The default vocabulary. Wherever a published language specification
names a concept, that name is used:

  - The PostScript Language Reference Manual (3rd ed.): setpagedevice
    and its parameters /PageSize, /HWResolution, /PageOffset,
    /ProcessColorModel, /Policies and /Install (PLRM3 section 6.2;
    /ProcessColorModel appears among the colour-separation page device
    parameters alongside /Separations and /SeparationColorNames,
    /Policies carries the recovery policies of section 6.2.7, and
    /Install the device initialisation of section 6.2.6); the
    /Separation and /DeviceN colour spaces; the %stdout special file.

  - The PDF specification (ISO 32000): the content-stream operators
    the pdfwrite device emits (cs/scn, k/K, rg/RG, path operators),
    the FunctionType 0 and 4 function dictionaries, the Type 4
    calculator operator subset, and #xx name escaping.

  - Adobe's pdfmark Reference: the /DOCINFO pdfmark.

Future separating raster output should follow the same rule: PLRM3
defines /Separations and /SeparationColorNames, so a separating device
builds on those rather than adopting another implementation's device
name or output-file naming scheme.


== 2. Xpost's own names ==

Everything internal is original to xpost or its contributors and
resembles no other implementation's API: the device method protocol
(Create, Destroy, Emit, PutPix, GetPix, DrawLine, DrawRect, FillRect,
FillPoly, FillPath, StrokePath, BlendPix, PaintImage, Erase), the
device keys /nativecolorspace, /dimensions, /VectorGlyphs,
/VectorSyntax, /.colormodels, /.registersep and /Private, the
dot-prefixed internal operators, and the device dictionary key
/OutputFileName (original to xpost, 2013).

PaintImage takes a whole sampled image -- its rows, the image-to-
device matrix, the decode ranges and the interpolation request -- so
a device that stores images rather than pixels receives one. It is
xpost's own method and mirrors nothing.

Devices whose names are xpost's own choices: null, svgwrite,
dscwrite, pgm, ppm, pbm, tiff. The raster names are the names of the
formats they emit. dscwrite is named for the structuring convention
its output follows (Adobe's Document Structuring Conventions), not
for any other implementation's device: the ps-writing devices of
other interpreters carry other names, deliberately not adopted.


== 3. Deliberate compatibility names ==

A small set of names is adopted from the vocabulary that existing
PostScript job streams in the field actually use, so that such jobs
run unmodified. Each is functionally required for that purpose; the
set is kept minimal and confined to the job-stream boundary (nothing
internal mirrors another implementation):

  - /OutputDevice     setpagedevice key selecting the device
  - /OutputFile       setpagedevice key naming the output path
                      (mapped at the boundary onto xpost's own
                      /OutputFileName)
  - /TextAlphaBits    device feature naming how many bits of a glyph's
                      edge coverage the device is sent
  - /WhiteIsOpaque    device feature making white marks opaque on the
                      transparent-page device
  - setcustomcolor, findcmykcustomcolor, findrgbcustomcolor,
    customcolorimage, separationimage
                      the separation vocabulary job streams use to name
                      a spot colour and paint with it; the colour itself
                      is priced as a Separation space of PLRM 4.8.4
  - setdistillerparams, currentdistillerparams
                      parameters a job stream sets when it expects to be
                      distilled; which of them act rather than being
                      recorded and handed back is in COMPLIANCE
  - pdfwrite, pngalpha, bbox    device names job streams select
  - MaxBandBytes, CurBandHeight, RecordSpill, CurRecordSpill
                      what one band of a page may cost, how deep a band
                      the budget bought, where a retained page's marks
                      are to be held and where the page in hand has
                      them. Reported by currentsystemparams beside the
                      parameters of PLRM Appendix C, and set by the
                      invocation rather than by a page description,
                      since Appendix G bars a program from choosing how
                      its marks are held.

                      The marks themselves are group 1: a held record is
                      what Appendix C calls a display list, and it is
                      reported as MaxDisplayList and CurDisplayList
                      under the names the specification gives. What is
                      left here is the raster band, which the
                      specification does not name because the band
                      device operators are obsolete -- so one figure is
                      reported twice, as the bytes a band of raster may
                      cost and as the bytes of display list that may be
                      held beside it.

Additions to this group should name the job-stream requirement that
forces them and should stay out of internal interfaces; anything a
published Adobe specification already names belongs in group 1
instead.
